WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.220
Animal cancer studies take years.

2
00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:08.480
How can bacteria on a dish screen a chemical for DNA mutation in a few days?

3
00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:13.160
Bruce Ames turned broken Salmonella into living detectors.

4
00:00:16.333 --> 00:00:21.333
The test uses Salmonella strains that cannot make the amino acid histidine.

5
00:00:22.693 --> 00:00:26.593
On a dish with trace histidine, cells divide a few times and stop.

6
00:00:27.913 --> 00:00:32.793
Only a new mutation restoring histidine synthesis lets a cell form a colony.

7
00:00:34.233 --> 00:00:37.573
Each colony is a revertant, marking one such mutation.

8
00:00:38.993 --> 00:00:43.413
Untreated dishes also grow a few revertants: the spontaneous background.

9
00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:50.680
Outside agents that cause mutations are mutagens, and different mutagens change DNA

10
00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:54.440
differently. So the test uses separate strains.

11
00:00:55.940 --> 00:01:01.440
TA100 reverts mainly by base-pair substitution, swapping one base pair for another.

12
00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:09.200
TA98 reverts mainly by frameshift, adding or removing bases not in multiples of

13
00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:10.200
three.

14
00:01:10.583 --> 00:01:14.083
Yet many carcinogens do nothing to bacteria alone.

15
00:01:15.663 --> 00:01:21.683
Benzo[a]pyrene binds DNA only after liver enzymes oxidize it into reactive products.

16
00:01:23.043 --> 00:01:27.203
So dishes run with and without S9, a rat-liver enzyme fraction.

17
00:01:28.503 --> 00:01:34.083
A rising revertant count with liver enzymes flags a bacterial mutagen, not human

18
00:01:34.083 --> 00:01:35.083
cancer.

19
00:01:35.667 --> 00:01:38.347
Test Compound X at three rising doses.

20
00:01:39.827 --> 00:01:44.847
Background is about 120 in TA100, 30 in TA98.

21
00:01:46.247 --> 00:01:51.447
Without S9, TA100 stays flat; so does TA98 with S9.

22
00:01:52.687 --> 00:01:59.087
Only TA100 with S9 climbs: 240, 480, 950.

23
00:02:00.807 --> 00:02:01.807
What does X do?

24
00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:09.040
Divide each count by background.

25
00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:13.160
240 over 120 is 2.

26
00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:17.980
480 is 4, and 950 is about 8.

27
00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:25.040
The rise needs liver enzymes, so a metabolite acts; X itself shows no detectable

28
00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:32.800
effect. TA100 reverts by base-pair substitution, so that metabolite causes base-pair

29
00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:34.660
substitutions in Salmonella.

30
00:02:35.683 --> 00:02:41.003
Always check four things in order: background, dose response, strains, and S9.

31
00:02:42.303 --> 00:02:46.563
A positive shows bacterial mutation, not cancer risk in people.

32
00:02:47.823 --> 00:02:49.803
A negative never proves safety either.

33
00:02:51.083 --> 00:02:56.103
Some carcinogens act without mutating DNA, or through processes bacteria lack.

34
00:02:57.167 --> 00:03:00.687
Once liver extract joined the dish, discoveries followed.

35
00:03:02.027 --> 00:03:09.107
In 1973, Ames and colleagues showed liver extract turned 18 tested carcinogens into

36
00:03:09.107 --> 00:03:10.227
frameshift mutagens.

37
00:03:11.587 --> 00:03:20.347
In 1975, Joyce McCann and colleagues found 156 of 174 carcinogens in their tested

38
00:03:20.347 --> 00:03:21.407
set mutagenic.

39
00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:25.040
The assay soon tested everyday products.

40
00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:34.740
In 1975, Ames, Kammen, and Yamasaki found 150 of 169 hair-dye formulations

41
00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:41.780
mutagenic. In 1978, Barry Commoner and colleagues found mutagens in cooked ground

42
00:03:41.780 --> 00:03:48.240
beef. Each dish flags bacterial mutation, while human cancer risk needs other

43
00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:48.680
evidence.
